rong diagonal move.\n- **(E, 6)**: This move places a piece at E6 and flips E5. While it looks quiet, it creates a vertical wall (E4-E5-E6) that invites the opponent to play at D6. A white move at D6 would flip my internal piece at D5, which is strategically bad.\n- **(F, 7)**: This move extends to the outer edges of the board too early. It gives up central control and is generally considered a weak opening move.\n\nReason: (C, 4) is the best choice because it consolidates my position in the center, connects my pieces efficiently, and forces the opponent into a defensive posture without creating immediate weaknesses in my own formation (unlike E6). It is preferred over D3 to minimize the opponent's diagonal control.\n\nAnswer: (C,4)"}
