ht side. It uses the anchor at H4 to flip the diagonal E7-F6-G5. This move extends my control of the right edge (where I already have F8, G8, H8) and consolidates my pieces in row G by connecting G4 and G6-G8.\n- **(F, 2)**: A quiet move that flips only one piece (E2). It is positionally sound but less ambitious than securing an edge.\n- **(A, 5)**: Takes a spot on the top edge. However, playing on an empty edge (Row A) can be risky as it might give the opponent tactical chances to stabilize that edge or access corners later.\n- **(G, 2)**: Flips G3 but opens up (G, 1) for the opponent, which would allow them to flip the G-row back. This is tactically poor.\n\nReason: (D, 8) is the strongest move because it secures an edge position and unites my isolated piece groups in the bottom-right into a single strong block. It does not give the opponent any immediate strong counter-attacks (like corner access) and restricts their mobility by removing their pieces from the diagonal.\n\nAnswer: (D, 8)"}
