onent to take this corner while trying to consolidate the remaining edges.\n\nEvaluation:\n- **(A, 2)**: This is a C-square move adjacent to the empty corner A1. If I play here, I flip pieces in row A. The opponent can immediately play A1 (sandwiching my new piece at A2 against my pieces at A8), capturing the corner and potentially sweeping the row. This is a blunder.\n- **(B, 7)**: This move flips C6. However, it places a piece adjacent to the empty square B8. This allows the opponent to play B8 (sandwiching B7 against B6), which destabilizes the right edge near my corner A8.\n- **(C, 8)**: This takes an edge piece but sets up the opponent to play B8 (sandwiching C8 and D8 against E8), which destroys my control of the bottom-right edge.\n- **(B, 1)**: This move captures C1, turning it into my piece. This connects B1 with my existing wall from D1 to H1, effectively giving me control of the entire left column (except A1). Since I occupy B1, the opponent cannot use column 1 to access A1. It does not give the opponent a valid move to take A1 immediately (opponent would need to play A2 first, which would give me A1). This is a structurally sound move.\n\nReason: (B, 1) is the best move because it secures the left edge of the board without exposing the A1 corner to the opponent. It prevents the opponent from utilizing the C1 square and restricts their mobility in the top-left quadrant, while other moves like (A, 2) give away the corner or (C, 8) weaken my existing edge control.\n\nAnswer: (B, 1)'}
